    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d57">

                <pb facs="0137" n="971" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg971"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 103.96/332</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Spain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Willard</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-03-13T20:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 13, 1918, 8 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>957. For Chadbourne [from War Trade Board]:</p>
                <p>No. 4. Embassy’s 1124, March 9, 11 a.m. United States of course claims
                    jurisdiction of boats Canal Zone. Ordinarily exercised by this Board where
                    bunkers or supplies requested or Trading with the Enemy Act involved, but our
                    jurisdiction might be invoked respecting cargoes if needed by United States. Do
                    not understand what you mean by your question, “What must owners do?”</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
